Network Connectivity Troubleshooting – First-Line Support

Overview

During my IT support experience at The Documented Warehouse,I assisted users with network and internet connectivity issues across multiple 
branches.
Troubleshooting was performed remotely through the helpdesk or with users/branch contacts when physical assistance was required.

Troubleshooting Approach

When a user reported a connectivity issue, I followed a structured troubleshooting process:

1. Identify the user and location -Confirm the affected user's details and branch/location.
                                  -Determine whether the issue affects one user or multiple users.

2.Establish the scope of the issue -Ask the user whether other users are experiencing the same problem.
                                   -Consider whether the issue may be workstation-specific or related to the branch network

3.Check the basic connection -Confirm that the workstation is connected to the network.
                             -Check whether the network connection appears active.
                             -Restart the workstation as an initial troubleshooting step.

4.Test internet access -Open a website to determine whether the connectivity issue persists.

5.Test local network connectivity - Ping the workstation's default gateway to determine whether it can communicate with the local network gateway.

6.Troubleshoot DNS and IP configuration -Check DNS-related connectivity where required.
                                        -Flush the DNS cache.
                                        -Release and renew the workstation's IP address.

7.Retest -Test the website again after making the changes.
         -Confirm whether connectivity has been restored.

8.Remote or onsite support -Continue troubleshooting remotely where possible.
                           -For branch users, work with the branch manager or an available technically capable user when physical checks are required.
                           -Escalate issues outside first-line support to the appropriate technical team.

